# tests/registry: the suites tests/run reads. One per line:
#
#   name|runtime tools|tier|images|memory|architectures
#
# The make target is `run-` and the name, so it is not written here. The runtime
# tools are the external commands the suite needs beyond the build toolchain, to
# build its own products or to run, separated by commas; `-` means none. An
# entry of choices, such as `a+b/c+d`, is met when the host has every command of
# one choice: here a and b, or c and d. A set of suites also needs the commands
# `make prepare-tools` prints, since it builds every suite's products before the
# first starts. The tier is `core` for the set `make test` runs and `full` for
# the rest; `tests/run all` runs both. The images are what the suite writes
# under build/images, as globs separated by commas, and the field is left out
# for a suite whose files are named after it: `NAME-test*`, `NAME.*`, `NAME-*`
# and `NAME/*`, and for a second-card twin, `NAME-e1000`, the plain suite's
# names with `-e1000` before the extension. The runner refuses a glob two suites
# declare, and fails a run in which a file was made that no suite of the run
# owns, or that two own, unless the build's own recorded products cover it, so
# this column is the inventory of what the suites touch. The memory is the most
# guest memory the suite's machines ask for at once, in MiB; left out, it is
# QEMU's default of 128. The runner admits a suite to the pool only while the
# running suites' memory plus its own fits the memory budget, so the machines a
# host can hold are what runs at once and not the worker count alone; a suite
# larger than the whole budget runs alone rather than never. The core tier is
# chosen by one rule: every subsystem of the kernel is exercised at least once.
# Through the pool it finishes when its longest member does.
#
# The architectures, i686 and x86_64 with commas between them, are the ones
# `tests/run` runs the suite on, for the ARCH it is given; left out, the suite
# runs on both. Among the runtime tools, `qemu` is the emulator of that
# architecture, qemu-system-i386 or qemu-system-x86_64, which `tests/run`
# looks for in its place, and an x86-64 run writes under build/x86_64/images
# what an i686 run writes under build/images.
#
# Order is the schedule order: the pool starts at the top, and a pool finishes
# when its longest member does, so the long suites are listed first.
